#
# Conformance case: teleology/fallback-combinator.
#
# A logic:Fallback program combinator (try-primary-else-alternate) is declared
# over two sub-programs: primaryRoute (left operand) is attempted first; if its
# executional entailment does not hold from the current state, backupRoute (right
# operand) is executed instead. This pins the structural vocabulary and the key
# distinction: logic:Fallback is a STRUCTURAL PROGRAM OPERATOR that routes
# control to an alternate when the primary cannot succeed, typed APART from
# action-level logic:compensation (which undoes the EFFECT of an action that ran
# and reached a realized nondeterministic outcome branch). Compensation recovers
# from an outcome that occurred; Fallback structurally provides an alternate path
# before commitment. The two sub-programs use logic:leftOperand (primary) and
# logic:rightOperand (alternate), reusing the same properties
# logic:SerialConjunction and logic:Choice use.
#
# CRITICAL: the teleology evaluator seeds ONLY from input.nq world facts; this
# input.logic.ttl carries the minimal, clean-compiling, certifiable program axiom
# the projections need (no asserted teleology triples here — the world facts live
# in input.nq, exactly as the foundation conformance cases keep theirs).

@prefix logic: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/> .
@prefix ex:    <https://example.org/teleology/fallback-combinator/> .

ex:GoalExpression a logic:GoalExpression .
